\section{Zero Knowledge $\cq$ Protocol}
\label{apdx:zk_cq}
We provide a zero knowledge version of the
$\cq$ \cite{cq} protocol. In \cite{MatrixLookup}
two highly optimized zero knowledge protocols (cq+, cq++) 
are presented (with proof size $7 \G_1 + 1 \F$).
Our protocol has the same asymptotic (faster concrete in certain
situations) prover cost,
a slightly higher proof size ($7\G_1 + 2\F$)
and verifier cost (one more pairing than cq++).
Compared with \cite{MatrixLookup} and the original $\cq$ protocol,
the advantage of our protocol is that the size of the query table (i.e., number
of lookups) does not have to be a factor of the scalar field size or the
lookup table size (e.g., de-factor power of $2$ as in \cite{MatrixLookup}). 
In some application scenarios, this is desirable as one does not have
to fill the query tables with ``dummy" entries to round up its size.
Then the concrete performance of the prover in our protocol can be
potentially $0.6\times$ faster, e.g., if the query table size is $2^k + 1$.
The main idea of our protocol is to use QA-NIZK for linear subspace
\cite{KW15,LegoSnarkFull}
to reason about sum of arrays, instead of relying on the Aurora Lemma \cite{aurora19},
which needs to work with a multiplicative subgroup of the scalar field.

\subsection{Problem Statement}
We use $\n$ to denote $n$ rounded up to the next
power of $2$ (e.g., $2048$ for $1025$).
Let $\myvec{T \in \F^N}$ be a secret lookup table of size $N$.
We define $T(X) = \sum_{i=1}^N \myvec{T}_i L_{N,i}(X)$ as its {\em encoding polynomial}.
Let $r_T$ be a random nonce and define $\hat{T}(X) = T(X) + z_\H(X)$, where
$z_\H(X) = X^N-1$ is the vanishing polynomial for all roots of unity of $\H$.
We say $[\hat{T}(s)]_1$ is the Pedersen (vector) commitment to $\myvec{T}$.
Similarly, given a query table $\myvec{t} \in \F^n$ (where $n$ is not necessarily
a power of $2$ that supports FFT), define
$t(X) = \sum_{i=1}^n \myvec{t}_i L_{\n,i}(X)$ and
$\hat{t}(X) = t(X) + r_t z_\Vtwo(X)$ for randomness $r_t$.
The vector commitment to $\myvec{t}$ is $[\hat{t}(s)]_1$.
Note that the computing cost of $[\hat{t}(s)]_1$,
in terms of group operations, is $n$ instead of $\n$ (even though
the Lagrange base polynomials are defined over domain of $\n$).
We often use $\Commit_{\myvec{T}}$ to denote $[\hat{T}(s)]_1$.
Similar notations apply to all other vectors and their encoding polynomials
in our protocol.

We denote the protocol as $\zkauxlookup$, which provides
a collection of algorithms as shown below. 

\begin{enumerate}
	\item $(\pkkzg,\vkkzg) \leftarrow \cqsetup(\secparam, N, n): $ a trusted set-up
	given security parameter $\secparam$ and lookup (query) table size
	$N (n)$, samples bilinear groups and generates the
	prover and verifier keys $(\pkkzg, \vkkzg)$ for the KZG 
	polynomial commitment \cite{Kate10}. We require $N$ and
	$\n$ (note: not $n$) to be a factor
	of $|\F|$ which allows FFT. 
	\footnote{To us it is still an unknown problem if the restriction on
	$N$ being a power of $2$ that allows FFT can be relaxed.
	It involves modifying the algorithm for batch computing
	$[Q_i(s)]_1$ using the amortized KZG proof algorithm (FK algorithm) 
	in \cite{FK23}.
	A factor polynomial of $z_{\Htwo}(X)/z_\H(X)$ needs to be applied
	to each quotient polynomial. An extension of the
	FK algorithm for it seems not straightforward, and the difficulty
	arises in applying the Toeplitz matrix.}

	\item $(\pk,\vk,\Commit_{\myvec{T}}) 
		\leftarrow \cqpreproc(\myvec{T}, r_T, \pkkzg,\vkkzg)$: The prover
	generates the auxiliary information 
	(e.g., the cached polynomials for $\cq$) 
	based on $\pkkzg$.
	It also returns 
	$\bT = [\hat{T}(s)]_1$, and also expands $\vkkzg$ with
	$[\hat{T}(s)]_2$. Note it
	does not have access of trapdoor.

	\item $(\bt, \prf)
	\leftarrow \cqprove(\pk, \myvec{t}, r_t)$: It produces
	a zero knowledge proof $\prf$ for
	$\myvec{t} \lookup \myvec{T}$, and 
	the vector commitment of $\myvec{t}$.

	\item $1/0 \leftarrow \cqverify(\vk, 
	\Commit_{\myvec{t}}, \prf):$ It checks that $\prf$ is valid.
	Note that the proof implies $\bt$ hides a vector of size $n$.

	\item $\prf 
	\leftarrow \cqfoldprove(\pk_{\myvec{U}}, 
		\pk_{\myvec{V}}, 
		\myvec{u}, \myvec{v}, \alpha)$: Given the 
		prover keys for two lookup tables $\myvec{U}$ and $\myvec{V}$,
		it produces $\prf$ for asserting that
		$\myvec{u} + \alpha \myvec{v} \lookup \myvec{U} + \alpha \myvec{V}$.
		It is required that both prover keys agree on the same
		$(N,n)$.
\end{enumerate}


% Note that different from the application context of
% general proof systems such as SublonK \cite{CCG23} where
% lookup is used, our protocol supports the case where
% both $\myvec{T}$ and $\myvec{t}$ are secret.
% Our technique assumes that $\Commit_{\myvec{T}}$ is permanent (e.g.,
% $\myvec{T}$ is permanent but secret and
% $\Commit_{\myvec{T}}$ is posted publicly
% in a instant asset proof \cite{FullIZPR}). 
% However, $\Commit_{\myvec{t}}$ is used and proved
% only one time. So for the same $\myvec{t}$, if it is to be proved
% again, the $\Commit_{\myvec{t}}$ should be re-generated
% using a fresh nonce.
% In the context of this paper (efficient zkregex proof), 
% $\myvec{T}$ is public, as the lookup tables for range proof and for finite state
% machine transition tables for public signature set.
% Here one can simply open $\Commit_{\myvec{T}}$.

\subsection{Adding Zero Knowledge}
We use two different techniques for lifting $\cq$ to zk, one bounded-leaky
and the other is complete zk.

\subsubsection{Bounded Leaky-Zk}
The idea is to mask a polynomial with a random polynomial.
It is formally defined as bounded-zk in Lunar \cite{Lunar},
but was unofficially used in PlonK \cite{GWC19},
\footnote{ 
NOTE: It was mentioned in Lunar's
introduction in bounded-leaky zk, 
however, didn't find where it is in \cite{GWC19}, {\bf CHECK LATER.}}
and then summarized in \cite[Section 3.6]{CCG23}.
This technique is used in three concurrent proposals in
making $\cq$ zero-knowledge \cite{CCG23,FullIZPR,MatrixLookup}.
We formally define it below.

\begin{lemma} Let $\myvec{f} \in \F^n$, and let 
$f(X) = \sum_{i=1}^n \myvec{f}_i \cdot L_{\n,i}(X)$.
Given a fixed $b>0$, we say that $\hat{f}$
is a {\em $b$-bounded-zk extension} of $f(X)$ 
in the sense that there exists a $b$-1 degree
random polynomial $R(X)$ s.t.
\[
	\hat{f}(X) = f(X) + R(X) z_{\Vtwo}(X)
\]
$\hat{f}$ satisfies the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item For $i\in [n]:$ $\hat{f}(\omega_{\n}^i) = \myvec{f}_i$, where
	$\omega_{\n}$ is the $\n$'th root of unity of $\F$.
	\item Fix $b$ {\em random} points. Given the evaluation of $\hat{f}$ 
		over these points,
		for any $\myvec{u} \in \F^{\n}$,
		there exists one and only one $b$-1 degree polynomial $U(X)$ s.t.
		$\hat{f}(X)$ = $u(X)$ + $U(X) z_{\Vtwo}(X)$, 
		where $u(X) = \sum_{i=1}^{\n} \myvec{u}_i \cdot L_{\n,i}(X)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

Property (2) essentially states that as long as there are no more than
$b$ evaluations, $\hat{f}$ can be generated from any arbitrary
vector (and since there is a one-to-one correspondence of the mask polynomial,
the distribution of $\myvec{u}$ is uniform), 
thus leaking no information about $\myvec{f}$.
Note that for the $1$-leaky case, $R(X)$ is degree $0$.

(Proof Sketch:) Property (1) is apparent in that
for any $i\in [n]$: $z_{\Vtwo}\left(\omega_{\n}^i\right) = 0$. Property (2)
is true because given $b$ random points $\{r_i\}_{i=1}^b$:
one has for each $i\in [b]: U(r_i) = (\hat{f}(r_i) - u(r_i))/z_{\Vtwo}(r_i)$.
Given that $r_i$ is randomly sampled, the probability that
$z_{\Vtwo}(r_i)=0$ is negligible and it is also unlikely there
are $i\neq j$ s.t. $r_i = r_j$. In this case, given
$b$ points, $U(X)$ can always be computed using Lagrange interpolation
and its degree is bounded by $b-1$, and it is the unique $b-1$ polynomial
that passes through these points.

{\bf Remark:} for bounded-leaky-zk to apply, $\hat{f}(X)$ has to
be evaluated at {\em random} points. If one evaluates $\hat{f}$
at known points, e.g., $\omega_{\n}^i$, it will still leak information
about $\myvec{f}$. In \cite{FullIZPR}, we rely on a univariate 
zk-VPD scheme which is introduced in \cite{zkvsql}.
In this paper, we introduce the use of QA-NIZK for a more efficient
construction.

\subsubsection{QA-NIZK \cite{KW15,LegoSnarkFull}}
In the $\cq$ protocol, given a vector $\myvec{A} \in \F^N$ and
$\myvec{B} \in \F^n$, there is a step which needs to prove
that $\sum_{i=1}^N \myvec{A}_i = \sum_{i=1}^n \myvec{B}_i$.
The Aurora lemma \cite{aurora19} is applied to reason about
this: let $A(X)$ and $B(X)$ be the encoding polynomial
for $\myvec{A}$ and $\myvec{B}$ respectively,
it is known that $A(0) = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^N \myvec{A}_i}{N}$, and similar
applies to $B(X)$. 
In \cite{FullIZPR}, zk is achieved using
zk-VPD scheme \cite{zkvsql} where the value of $A(0)$ hides
in a Pedersen commitment. In \cite{MatrixLookup},
an additional $C(X)$ is defined ``roughly" as
$A(X) - \frac{n\cdot z_\H(X)}{N\cdot z_\V(X)}B(X)$, and Aurora lemma is applied again (but in zero
knowledge) to reason about $C(X)$. This approach results in the most
efficient zero knowledge $\cq$ protocol so far ($7\G_1 + 1\F$ proof,
$8n$ group operations for prover cost and $6$ pairings).

In this paper,
we leverage the QA-NIZK technique, which provides a conceptually
easier and even more efficient prover (in situations where
$n \neq \n$).
In particular,
our technique allows $n$ (query table size) not a power of two,
and $n$ does not have to be a factor of $N$ (which is
required by the techniques of \cite{MatrixLookup}).

Our starting point is the quasi-adaptive NIZK (QA-NIZK)
for linear subspace of vector of group elements \cite{KW15}.
Given $\myvec{M} \in \G_1^{n\times t}$, and
$\myvec{y} \in \G_1^{n}$, the technique
proves the knowledge of a secret vector $\myvec{x} \in \F^t$
s.t. $\myvec{y} = \myvec{M} \myvec{x}$. In \cite{KW15},
there is a restriction that $n>t$ (the number of equations
needs to be greater than the number of variables). This restriction is
removed in LegoSnark \cite[Appendix D]{LegoSnarkFull}, 
which also ports the proof to the algebraic group model (AGM). We now apply
the LegoSnark variation, and provides the zk-SNARK
for the following relation:

\begin{small}
\begin{eqnarray*}
 & & \prfqa(N,n,\Commit_{\myvec{A}}, \Commit_{\myvec{B}}) 
 \big\{
 (\myvec{A}, \myvec{B}, r_a, r_b):  \\
 & & 
  \hspace*{0.2in}
  \Commit_{\myvec{A}} = \sum_{i=1}^N \myvec{A}_i [L_{N,i}(s)]_1 +
 	r_a [z_\H(s)]_1  \And  \\
 & & 
  \hspace*{0.2in}
   \Commit_{\myvec{B}} = \sum_{i=1}^n \myvec{B}_i [L_{\n,i}(s)]_1 +
 	r_b [z_\Vtwo(s)]_1 \And  \\
 & & 
  \hspace*{0.2in}
   \sum_{i=1}^N \myvec{A}_i = \sum_{i=1}^n \myvec{B}_i  \\
 & & 
 \big\}
\end{eqnarray*}
\end{small}

Intuitively, $\prfqa$ states that the sum of
two committed vectors are the same, and note that
it explicitly asserts the vector size (degree bound)
of the two vectors. 

We present the construction for $\prfqa$ 
in Figure\ \ref{fig_qa}. 
Matrix $\myvec{M}$ has three rows.
The first row encodes the relation for generating
$[\hat{A}(s)]_1$, and the second for $[\hat{B}(s)]_1$,
and the third produces  $[0]_1$ if the
sum of $\myvec{A}$ is equal to that of
$\myvec{B}$. Notice that in 
$\ProveQANIZK$, the
witness $\myvec{w}$ is the concatenation of 
$\myvec{A}$, its random nonce $r_a$,
$\myvec{B}$, and its random nonce $r_b$. 

\def\hp{\hspace*{0.1in}}
\begin{small}
\begin{algorithm}[bt]
\SetKwData{Left}{left}\SetKwData{This}{this}\SetKwData{Up}{up}
\SetKwFunction{Union}{Union}\SetKwFunction{FindCompress}{FindCompress}
$ (\pk,\vk) \leftarrow \SetupQANIZK(N,n,s)$
\newline
Define $M \in \F^{3\times(N+n+2)}$ as following: \newline
\begin{small}
$\left(
  \begin{array}{l}
    ({[}L_{N,i}(s)]_1)_{i=1}^N, [z_\H(s)]_1, ([0])_{i=1}^{n+1}\ \\ 
    ({[}0]_1)_{i=1}^{N+1}, ([L_{\n,i}]_1)_{i=1}^n, [z_\Vtwo(s)]_1 \\ 
	({[}1]_1)_{i=1}^{N}, [0]_1, ([|\F|-1]_1)_{i=1}^{n}, [0]_1
  \end{array}
\right)$
\end{small}
\newline
Sample $\myvec{k} \in \F^3$, $a \in \F$.\newline
Compute $\myvec{P} \leftarrow \myvec{M}^\intercal \myvec{k}
\in \G_1^{N+n+2}$,
$\myvec{C} = a \cdot [\myvec{k}]_2 \in \G_2^3$.  \newline
Let $\pk = \myvec{P}$ and $\vk=(\myvec{C}, [a]_2)$.
Return $(\pk,\vk)$.
\\
$(\myvec{x},\prf) \leftarrow 
	\ProveQANIZK(\myvec{A},\myvec{B}, \pk)$:  \newline
	Sample $r_a, r_b$ from $\F$ and 
	define $\myvec{w} = \myvec{A} \concat r_a \concat \myvec{B} \concat r_b \in \F^{N+n+2}$. 
	\newline
	Compute $[\hat{A}(s)] \leftarrow \sum_{i=1}^{N} \myvec{M}_{1,i} \myvec{A}_i
		+ \myvec{M}_{1,N+1} r_a \in \G_1$. \newline
	Compute $[\hat{B}(s)] \leftarrow \sum_{i=1}^{n} \myvec{M}_{2,i+N+1} 
		\myvec{B}_i
		+ \myvec{M}_{2,N+n+2} r_b \in \G_1$. \newline
	Compute $\prf \leftarrow \myvec{w}^\intercal \myvec{P} \in \G_1$.\newline
	Let $\myvec{x} = ([\hat{A}(s)]_1, [\hat{B}(s)]_1, [0]_1) \in \G_1^3$. \newline
	Return $(\myvec{x}, \prf)$.
\\
$1/0 \leftarrow \VerifyQANIZK(([\hat{A}(s)]_1,[\hat{B}(s)]_1),
	\prf,\vk):$ \newline
	Let $\myvec{x} = ([\hat{A}(s)]_1, [\hat{B}(s)]_1, [0]_1) \in \G_1^3$. \newline
	Return $1$ if and only if
	$
		\prod_{i=1}^{3} e(\myvec{x}_i, \myvec{C}_i)
		= e(\prf, [a]_2)
	$

\caption{QA-NIZK for 
$\sum_{i=1}^N \myvec{A}_i = \sum_{i=1}^n \myvec{B}_i$.}
\label{fig_qa}
\end{algorithm}
\end{small}

\begin{lemma}
\label{lemma_qanizk}
\cite[Appendix D]{LegoSnarkFull}
Under the discrete logarithm assumption in 
AGM, the construction in 
Figure\ \ref{fig_qa} is perfectly complete,
computationally knowledge sound, and
perfectly zero knowledge.
\end{lemma}

Lemma \ref{lemma_qanizk} directly follows
Theorem D.1 in \cite[Appendix D]{LegoSnarkFull},
because the distribution of $\myvec{M}$ is
witness sampleable.

{\bf Efficiency:} When the protocol is used in
the zk-$\cq$ protocol, the prover cost is $2n$ because
$\myvec{A}$ is sparse (up to $n$ non-zero entries).
The verifier cost is actually $3$ pairings, because
the last entry in $\myvec{x}$ in $\VerifyQANIZK$
is $[0]_1$, and its pairing can be skipped.
% We note in particular, that in the application
% scenarios that $n$ is not a power of $2$,
% there is no need to fill the query table with
% dummy entries. This can cut prover cost significantly
% because no need to round $n$ up
% (e.g., from $1025$ to $2048$).

{\bf Remark:} When the QA-NIZK protocol is used in
zk-$\cq$, the adversary that attacks knowledge soundness
will have access of extra $\G_1$ elements:
$([s^i])_{i=0}^N$ (in addition to the group elements
in $\myvec{M}$ and $\myvec{P}$). This will not increase
the power of adversary in AGM. We provide a brief proof
below.

Let $\myvec{z} \in \G_1^{2N+2n+7}$ 
be the collection of group elements
collected from $\myvec{M}$ and $\myvec{P}$.
Let $\myvec{u} = ([s^i]_1)_{i=1}^N$. Assume that
the adversary can construct a false 
proof $\prf$ given $\myvec{z}$ and $\myvec{u}$
s.t. $\VerifyQANIZK(\Commit_\myvec{A}, \Commit_\myvec{B}, \prf)$
returns $1$ but 
$\sum_{i=1}^N \myvec{A}_i \neq \sum_{i=1}^n \myvec{B}_i$.
We argue that the same $\prf$ can be produced using
$\myvec{z}$ only.

By the AGM model, there exist
$\myvec{a} \in \F^{2N+2n+7}$ and
$\myvec{b} \in \F^{N}$ s.t.
$\prf = \sum_{i=1}^{2N+2n+7} \myvec{a}_i \myvec{z}_i  
 + \sum_{i=1}^{N} \myvec{b}_i \myvec{u}_i$.
Note that $\sum_{i=1}^N \myvec{b}_i \myvec{u}_i$ 
is a KZG commitment to an $N$-degree polynomial $b(X)$.
Then $b(X)$ can always be re-written
as $\sum_{i=1}^{N} \myvec{c}_i L_{N,i}(X) + \myvec{c}_{N+1} z_\H(X)$.
Then merge array $\myvec{c}$ into
$\myvec{a}$ for the corresponding keys in
$\myvec{z}$, one obtains $\prf$
using $\myvec{z}$ only. 



