\begin{theorem} \label{thm_main}
Under the Q-DLOG assumption in the AGM model and assuming
the Random Oracle Model, the construction presented in Figure\ \ref{fig_zkcq}
is perfectly complete, computational knowledge sound, and 
perfectly zero knowledge.
\end{theorem}


\begin{proof} 
{\bf Zero Knowledge:} The proof for completeness is trivial. We first
prove the perfectly zero knowledge property.
The simulator samples trap-door $s, \myvec{k}, a$
and constructs the $\pkkzg, \vkkzg$ using
$\cqsetup()$ (without using $\cqpreproc())$.  
The simulator is then given $\Commit_{\myvec{T}}$
and $\Commit_{\myvec{t}}$, and tries to produce a transcript
for proving $\myvec{t} \lookup \myvec{T}$ without knowing
$\myvec{t}$ and $\myvec{T}$.

The simulator first samples random $N$-degree polynomials:
$\hat{m}(X),$ $\hat{A}(X)$, and $n$-degree polynomial 
$\hat{B}(X)$, and verifier challenges $\beta$, $\gamma$, and $\eta$. Then the
simulator computes the following using trapdoor $s$:
$\bm \leftarrow [\hat{m}(s)]_1$,
$\ba \leftarrow [\hat{A}(s)]_1$,
$\bb \leftarrow [\hat{B}(s)]_1$. Note that the simulator
knows for example $\hat{m}(s)$.
Let $\myvec{x} = (\ba, \bb, [0]_1) \in \G_1^{3}$, and $\myvec{k}$
the trapdoor in QA-NIZK. 
Compute $\prfqa \leftarrow \myvec{k}^\intercal  \myvec{x}$.  
The simulator also computes $Z_\gamma = z_\V(\gamma)$.

Let $U = (\Commit_{\myvec{T}}$,
$\Commit_{\myvec{t}}$,
$\ba$, $\bb$, $\bm, \prfqa, \beta, \gamma, \eta)$. One can see that
elements of $U$ are independent of each other and
uniformly distributed. This is the same as the real distribution
of an honest verifier communicating with a prover.
We now show that $U$ uniquely decides the rest of the elements
in the proof. 
The simulator then computes the following with constant cost: \newline
$\bqa \leftarrow (A(s) 
\Commit_{\myvec{T}} + \beta \ba - \bm)z_\H(s)^{-1}$. \newline
$B_\gamma \leftarrow \hat{B}(\gamma)$. \newline
$\bqb \leftarrow \left(
\hat{B}(s)(\Commit_{\myvec{t}} + [\beta]_1) - [1]_1
\right) z_\V(s)^{-1}$.
\newline
$\bp \leftarrow \frac{[\hat{B}(s) - B_\gamma]_1 + 
\eta \left(B_\gamma \left(\bt + [\beta]_1\right)-[1]_1 - z_\V(s) \bqb \right)
+ \eta^2(z_\V(s) - Z_\gamma)
	}{s-\gamma}$. \newline

Apparently $(\bqa, B_\gamma, \bqb, \bp)$ has 
the same distribution as the real transcripts, as they are
also uniquely determined by $U$ in real conversations.

{\bf Knowledge Soundness:} 
We now follow the notations in $\cqverify()$, i.e.,
the proof is a tuple $(\bm, \ba, ..., Z_\gamma)$.
By the Q-DLOG assumption and
ideal testing lemma in $\cq$ \cite{cq}, one can see that all group elements
in the proof can be represented as a multi-variate polynomial
over variables $X, K_1, K_2, K_3$ (corresponding to trapdoor
$s,\myvec{k}_1, \myvec{k_2}, \myvec{k_3}$). This is different
from the original $\cq$ protocol because we've introduced extra
trapdoor $\myvec{k}$ in the QA-NIZK prover keys.

However, from QA-NIZK proof (Equation (2) in $\cqverify$)
we know that $\ba$ and $\bb$ in the proof represents two univariate polynomials
$\hat{A}(X)$ and $\hat{B}(X)$ of degree $N$ and $\n$.
One could probably also prove that 
$\hat{m}(X)$ and $\hat{Q_B}(X)$ are univariate polynomials via
QA-NIZK, but for saving prover cost, we
will discharge them via formal proof below.

We first consider $\hat{Q_B}(X)$ in Check (3) of $\cqverify$. 
Following the same argument
in \cite{MatrixLookup}, because $\eta$ is a random verifier
challenge, by Schwartz-Zippel, with high probability
$(X-\gamma)$ divides (1) $B(X) - B_\gamma$, (2)
$z_\V(X) - Z_\gamma$, and (3) $D(X,K_1,K_2,K_3)$.
We then know that $B_\gamma = \hat{B}(\gamma)$
and $Z_\gamma = z_\V(\gamma)$.
We then have: given fixed $\hat{t}(X, K_1, K_2, K_3)$ and
$\hat{Q_B}(X, K_1, K_2, K_3)$, and given random $\gamma$ (because
in ROM it is a verifier challenge raised after
commitment to $\hat{t}$ and $\hat{Q_B}$ are sent),
there exists $B_\gamma$ and $P(X, K_1, K_2, K_3)$ s.t.

\begin{small}
\begin{eqnarray} \label{eq_px}
& &
B_\gamma(\hat{t}(X, K_1, K_2, K_3) + \beta) - 1 
- z_\V(\gamma) \hat{Q_B}(X, K_1, K_2, K_3)  \nonumber\\
&=& P(X,K_1,K_2,K_3) (X - \gamma) 
\end{eqnarray}
\end{small}

Consider Equation\ \ref{eq_px}:
all multivariate polynomials are {\em semi-multi-linear} in the
way that the maximum degree of $K_1, K_2, K_3$ in any term of
the polynomial is up to $1$ (but $X$ can be up to $\n$, determined
by the group elements in $\myvec{P})$. 
% We now argue that
% these polynomials are all univariate, because given
% {\em fixed} $\hat{t}(X, K_1, K_2, K_3)$ and
% $\hat{Q_B}(X, K_1, K_2, K_3)$, given a random challenge
% $\gamma$, the probability is negligible to find
% $B_\gamma$ and $P(X, K_1, K_2, K_3)$ to make Equation\ \ref{eq_px}
% stand, if $\hat{t}(X,K_1,K_2,K_3)$ and
% $\hat{Q_B}(X, K_1, K_2, K_3)$ are not univariate polynomials
% over $X$. We provide the proof below.

We write $\hat{t}(X, K_1, K_2, K_3) + \beta$ as the following:
\[
	\sum_{x=0}^{\n} \sum_{k_1=0}^{1} \sum_{k_2=0}^{1} \sum_{k_3=0}^1
		\coeft_{x,k_1, k_2, k_3} X^{x} {K_1}^{k_1} {K_2}^{k_2} {K_3}^{k_3}
\]
Here $\coeft_{x,k_1,k_2,k_3}$ represents
the co-efficient of each term. Let $\vec{k} = (k_1, k_2, k_3)$,
we write as $\coeft_{x,\vec{k}}$ for short. Similarly
we use $\coefqb$ as the coefficients for
$\hat{Q_B}(X,K_1,K_2,K_3)$ and
$\coefp$ for
$P(X,K_1,K_2,K_3)$. It is known that the max degree for $X$
in these polynomials are $\n$, $\n$ and $\n-1$.
We write $z_\V(\gamma) = \sum_{i=0}^n z_i \gamma^i$, where
$z_i$ are publicly known (fixed) constants.

We now reason about the co-efficients related to those $K_i$
variables.  Fix $\vec{k}$ to be a non-zero vector (which implies that one of
$K_1$, $K_2$, $K_3$ appears). We study the relation between these
co-efficients. One can easily infer from Equation\ \ref{eq_px}
that:
\[
\begin{cases*}
 \bg\coeft_{x,\veck} + \sum_{i=0}^{\n} z_i\gamma^i \coefqb_{x,\veck} = -\gamma \coefp_{x,\veck}  & if ~ $x = 0$ \\
 \bg\coeft_{x,\veck} + \sum_{i=0}^{\n} z_i\gamma^i \coefqb_{x,\veck} = \coefp_{x-1,\veck} - \gamma \coefp_{x,\veck} & if ~$0<x< \n$ \\
 \bg\coeft_{x,\veck} + \sum_{i=0}^{\n} z_i\gamma^i \coefqb_{x,\veck} = \coefp_{x-1,\veck} & if ~ $x=\n$ \\
\end{cases*}
\]

Remove $\coefp$ elements from the above equation set, one obtains the 
equation over $\gamma$, which is a free variable.
To make the following equation hold with high
probability, all co-efficients
have to be zero.

\begin{eqnarray} 
0 & = &
\sum_{i=1}^{n} 
  \left( \gamma^{\n+i} \sum_{j=i}^{n} z_j 
  	\coefqb_{\n+i-j,\veck} \right) +
\label{line1} \\
& &\sum_{i=0}^{\n} \left( 
	\gamma^{i} \left(
		B_\gamma \coeft_{i,\veck} + 
		\sum_{j=0}^{i} z_j \coefqb_{i-j,\veck} \right)
	\right )
\label{line2} 
\end{eqnarray}

% Now consider the co-efficient for term $\gamma^{\n+n}$, it is
% $z_n \coefqb_{\n}$. Note that $z_n$ is the the lead
% co-efficient (for the highest degree term in $z_\V(X)$),
% and it cannot be zero. This enforces $\coefqb_{\n}$ be zero.
% Then following all terms in Line (\ref{line1}) in the above equation
% set, i.e., from detree $\n+n$ to $\n+1$, we reached:
% \begin{equation} \label{line3}
%   \forall i\in [\n-n+1,\n]: \coefqb_{i,\veck} = 0
% \end{equation}
% 
% Plugging Equation\ \ref{line3} into Line (\ref{line2}) we have
% the following:
Now consider Line (\ref{line2}): we have:
\begin{equation} \label{eq_main}
  \forall i \in [0,\n]: B_\gamma \coeft_{i,\veck} + 
  	\sum_{j=0}^i{z_j \coefqb_{i-j,\veck}}
\end{equation}

Observe Equation \ref{eq_main}, if $\coeft_{i,\veck}$ is 
not zero, this fixes the value 
of $\hat{B}(\gamma) = 
-\frac{\sum_{j=0}^i {z_j \coefqb_{i-j,\veck}}}{\coeft_{i,\veck}}$
for each $i\in [\n]$,
given that $\gamma$ is sampled after
the value of 
$\left(z_i, \coefqb_{i,\veck}, \coeft_{i,\veck}\right)_{i=0}^\n$
are commited in the proof and the claim.
The probability is negilible to
have $\hat{B}(X)$ being a $0$-degree polynomial
so that it evaluates to a constant for 
any random $\gamma$.
This then enforces $\coeft_{i,\veck}$ for
all $i\in [\n]$ and all non-zero $\veck$.
This implies that $\hat{t}$ is a univariate polynomial,
which implies $\hat{Q_B}$ is univariate as well.


We then examine check (1) of $\cqverify()$.
It is already known that $\ba$ hides univariate polynomials,
but not for $\bqa$ and $\bm$. By AGM model,
$\bqa$ can be written as $\hat{Q_A}(X) + \hat{Q_A'}(X, K_1, K_2, K_3)$
where $\hat{Q_A'}$ includes all terms with at least one of 
$K_i$ variables appearing (thus, the constant term belongs to $\hat{Q_A}$).
Similarly, $\bm$ can be split into two polynomials. Then from
Equation (1) of $\cqverify()$ we have:

\begin{eqnarray} \label{eq_a1}
& &\hat{A}(X)(\hat{T}(X) + \beta) - (\hat{m}(X) + \hat{m'}(X,K_1,K_2,K_3)) 
\nonumber \\
&=& (\hat{Q_A}(X) + \hat{Q_A'}(X,K_1,K_2,K_3))z_\H(X)
\end{eqnarray}

For Equation\ \ref{eq_a1} to hold, the polynomial of LHS-RHS must be
a zero (multi-variate) polynomial. Then we have both of the following
should be zero polynomial by separating the monorails by degrees
on $K_i$ variables.

\begin{small}
\begin{eqnarray}
& &\hat{A}(X)(\hat{T}(X) + \beta) - \hat{m}(X) 
- \hat{Q_A}(X)z_\H(X) \label{eq_main_a} \\
& & \hat{m'}(X,K_1,K_2,K_3)  + \hat{Q_A'}(X,K_1,K_2,K_3)z_\H(X)
\end{eqnarray}
\end{small}

From Equation\ \ref{eq_main_a}, we obtain: there exists
$\myvec{m},\myvec{A},\myvec{T} \in \F^N$
s.t.
\begin{eqnarray} \label{eq_a}
& & \forall 1\leq i\leq N: 
	\myvec{A}_i = \frac{\myvec{m}_i}{\myvec{T}_i + \beta} \\
& & \ba = \left[\sum_{i=1}^N \myvec{\myvec{A}_i} 
	L_{N,i}(s) + r_A z_\H(s)\right]_1 \\
& & \bm = \left[\sum_{i=1}^N 
	\myvec{\myvec{m}_i} L_{N,i}(s) + r_m z_\H(s)\right]_1 
\end{eqnarray}

Then using the QA-NIZK proof in (2) of $\cqverify()$
we reach the conclusion that the sum of $\myvec{A}$ is the same
as $\myvec{B}$, which concludes the soundness proof.
The knowledge extractor is provided directly by
the AGM model.
\end{proof}

{\bf Concrete Cost:} It takes $n$ group operations
to compute each of 
$[\hat{m}(s)]_1$,
$[\hat{A}(s)]_1$,
$[\hat{Q_A}(s)]_1$, and
$[\hat{B}(s)]_1$.
It takes $2n$ for $\prfqa$, as $\myvec{A}$ is sparse (i.e.,
it has no more than $n$ non-zero elements).
It takes $\n$ to compute each of
$[\hat{Q_B}(s)]_1$, and
$[P(s)]_1$.
The total prover cost is $6n + 2\n$ group operations.
The verifier cost is $7$ pairings.


\subsection{Fold Prove}
We briefly discuss the implementation details and cost.
Formally, the operation is defined as: 
\begin{small}
\[
\prf \leftarrow \cqfoldprove(\pk_{\myvec{U}}, 
		\pk_{\myvec{V}}, 
		\myvec{u}, \myvec{v}, \alpha)
\]
\end{small}

It produces
a zero knowledge proof $\prf$ for
$\myvec{u} + \alpha \myvec{v} \lookup 
\myvec{U} + \alpha \myvec{V}$.
Looking in the perspective of a ``single" lookup argument,
$\myvec{T} = \myvec{U} + \alpha \myvec{V}$
and $\myvec{t} = \myvec{u} + \alpha \myvec{v}$. 
We briefly discuss each of the steps of the prove function.

	The calculation of $\myvec{m}$ can be applied to just 
	$(\myvec{U}, \myvec{u})$, as the folding require that 
	$\myvec{m}$ would be the same for $(\myvec{V}, \myvec{v})$.
	Compute
	$\myvec{A}_i \leftarrow \myvec{m}_i/(\myvec{U}_i + \alpha\myvec{V} + \beta)$
	and similarly for $\myvec{B}_i$. Both can be computed
	using the same cost
	as the original $\prove$. The computation of
	$\ba$, $\bb$, $\bqb$, $\bp$ are also the same.

	The tricky part is how to compute $\bqa$. Consider how the
	quotient polynomials are computed:
	\begin{eqnarray*}
	& & L_{N,i}(X) U(X) = Q_{i,U}(X) z_\H(X)  + R_{i,U}(X)\\
	& & L_{N,i}(X) V(X) = Q_{i,V}(X) z_\H(X)  + R_{i,V}(X)
	\end{eqnarray*}

	They are homomorphic and quickly leads to:
	\begin{small}

	\begin{eqnarray*}
	& & L_{N,i}(X) (U(X) + \alpha V(X))= (Q_{i,U}(X) + \alpha Q_{i,V}(X)) z_\H(X)  \\
	& & ~~~	+ (R_{i,U}(X) + \alpha R_{i,V}(X))
	\end{eqnarray*}
	\end{small}

	Then $[Q_i(s)]_1$ can be computed as
	$[Q_{i,U}(s)]_1 + \alpha[Q_{i,V}(s)]_1$, and then
	based on which $[Q_{\hat{A}}]_1$ can be computed.
